issue_context.body:
#73803 (7.0) introduced a race condition when recreating the internal `Regex` list.
The issue is that we update the `IsChanged` flag before we replace the `_regexBypassList` field [here](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/4da3eae7a54a4a6abeb59df4953b6de4c65b53ea/src/libraries/System.Net.WebProxy/src/System/Net/WebProxy.cs#L128-L145).
If two threads query `GetProxy` or `IsBypassed` at the same time, one may not observe changes to the bypass list and return the wrong answer.

```c#
var proxy = new WebProxy("myProxy:1234");
proxy.BypassArrayList.Add("contoso");

var task1 = Task.Run(() => proxy.IsBypassed(new Uri("http://contoso")));
var task2 = Task.Run(() => proxy.IsBypassed(new Uri("http://contoso")));

if (await task1 != await task2)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Oh no");
}
```

This is limited to cases where the user modified the bypass list via the `BypassArrayList` property, which is hopefully rare.

comments:
> is the issue here more that because it's lazily initialized this can happen even without concurrent user modification of the list?

Yes.

```c#
var proxy = new WebProxy("myProxy:1234");
proxy.BypassArrayList.Add("contoso");

myHandler.Proxy = proxy;
// ... errors can occur when making requests
```

review_comments:
I think it'd be worth adding a comment as to why this is volatile, since that's a bit odd in a type that's not intended to be used concurrently, e.g.
```suggestion
            // While this type isn't intended to mutated concurrently with reads, non-concurrent updates
            // to the list might result in lazy initialization, and it's possible concurrent requests could race
            // to trigger that initialization.
            public volatile bool IsChanged;
```

The sort of transformation I had in mind was something like
```c#
Regex[] regexBypassList = _regexBypassList;
if (_bypassList is ChangeTrackingArrayList bypassList && bypassList.IsChanged)
{
    UpdateRegexList();
    regexBypassList = _regexBypassList; // It could have changed - read it again
}

if (regexBypassList is Regex[] regexBypassList)
```

Again, I have no idea if this would ever happen in practice, but I could imagine a CPU/PGO optimization that sees the `IsChanged` branch is almost never taken and decides to prefetch `_regexBypassList`.
This doesn't impact behavior for a single thread but is a race condition if multiple threads are involved.

From our code's perspective, I think it's okay to be more defensive in such cases (e.g. making the field `volatile` instead of only doing a `Volatile.Write` on it).
